# Line endings for the GENERATED launchers (field report 2026-08-07, defect 2).
#
# This file is tracked, so the installer's `git ls-files` copy carries it into every brain
# it creates — which is where it does its work: the brain commits its own launch.cmd /
# launch.sh, and `rehydrate.mjs` does not regenerate them on a second machine. Without a
# stated rule, a clone gets whatever the machine's core.autocrlf happens to be, and the
# generator's careful bytes are rewritten behind its back.
#
# The two families need OPPOSITE things, which is exactly how one rule ends up wrongly
# applied to both:
#
#   .cmd — cmd.exe re-seeks a batch file BY BYTE OFFSET as it runs. With LF-only endings
#          the accounting drifts and it resumes MID-TOKEN: the field stderr showed `REM`
#          executed as 'M', plus 'lf-heal' and 'epends', fragments of our own comments.
#
#   .sh  — sh treats a trailing CR as part of the token, so a CRLF launcher dies on an
#          invisible byte with an unreadable "command not found".
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Append-only notes merge by UNION (plan #84, live sync between machines). Two brains
# — a second machine, or a second person on the same private repo — routinely append
# to the SAME daily note or actions log. Git's built-in `union` driver resolves such a
# hunk by keeping both sides, no markers, no human: exactly the "keep both" the `/sync`
# skill recommends by hand for append-only vault content.
#
# ⚠️ NARROWED on 2026-09-03 (#84 step 4bis). The rule used to read `vault/**/*.md`,
# i.e. EVERY note. Union is not a merge, it is a CONCATENATION: it keeps both sides'
# lines with no marker and no question asked. That is the right resolution for a ledger
# nobody rewrites, and the wrong one for a page two people edit — there it silently
# interleaves two versions of one paragraph, and on the frontmatter it produces a note
# carrying `updated:` TWICE, which the indexer then refuses to read. That last case was
# caught after the fact (the sync tick re-checks a merged note's header and undoes the
# rebase); narrowing the rule means curated notes never reach that state at all.
#
# So the scope follows the vault's own vocabulary: the RAW CAPTURE ZONES — the same
# list `scripts/lib/wiki-lint.mjs` exempts from the orphan and frontmatter rules.
# `notes-union-merge.test.mjs` asserts the two lists agree, because a rule written down
# twice drifts (CONVENTIONS §5quater). Everywhere else in the vault a double edit stops
# the rebase, which is NOT a failure: the tick aborts, names the files, and the brain
# guides the merge at the next message (scripts/lib/remote-sync.mjs).
#
# `**/` matches zero or more directories, so each rule covers the vault root AND a
# universe subtree (`vault/acme/daily/…`) — the blind spot those zone lists have their
# own hardening plan for.
vault/**/daily/**/*.md merge=union
vault/**/raw-sources/**/*.md merge=union
vault/**/inbox/**/*.md merge=union
vault/**/_inbox/**/*.md merge=union
vault/**/actions-log.md merge=union
